From: Kali NetHunter rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: pin pm6125 L11 (UFS VCCQ2) at 1.8 V

The &rpm_requests block was taken from sm6375-sony-xperia-murray-pdx225.dts,
the only other board using this SoC when the port began, and L11 came across
with Sony's wide range, 1620 mV to 1980 mV. Mainline's RPM regulator driver
never reads the hardware, and no consumer of this rail votes a voltage:
ufshcd only calls regulator_enable() and regulator_set_load() over vccq2, and
the WCD9370 only enables its vdd-rxtx/vdd-px. So the framework settles on the
range's floor. With a min of 1620000 declared against the pm660_ht_lvpldo
range (base 1504000, 8 mV steps) the selector rounds up to 1624000 uV:

	l11    enabled   1624000 uV

L11 feeds two things at once, and both want 1.8 V:

	ufshc  vccq2-supply     = <&pm6125_l11>   (UFS I/O rail)
	wcd9370 vdd-rxtx/vdd-px = <&pm6125_l11>   (codec I/O)

1.624 V is below the 1.7 V minimum UFS VCCQ2 is specified for, so the storage
has been running out of spec since the port began -- a VCCQ2 below its floor
degrades the UFS link's I/O margins and can show up as CRC errors, gear
drops, UIC line errors, or silent data corruption in transit. The vendor
pins it with qcom,init-voltage = <1800000> in holi-regulators-pm6125.dtsi
(as L11A); mainline has no such property, so the constraint itself has to
say it. Narrowing to min == max is what makes the framework vote 1.8 V, the
same fix applied to L9 in the earlier "rhodep: pin pm6125 L9 at 1.8 V" patch.

This raises the rail, it never lowers it, so the codec I/O that shares L11
is only moved from 1.624 V up toward its own 1.8 V nominal.

Not yet confirmed by measurement on the device; this is a fix by inspection
against the vendor tree and the JEDEC UFS spec. L24 (UFS VCC) has the same
origin and is worth the same treatment once this one is validated.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
@@ -300,9 +300,21 @@
 			regulator-max-microvolt = <1980000>;
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * 1.8 V, not the 1624 mV the bottom of the PMIC's range gives.
+		 *
+		 * L11 is UFS VCCQ2 (and the WCD9370's I/O). Declaring the
+		 * part's full range and letting the framework pick means it
+		 * picks the minimum, which rounds up to 1624 mV -- below the
+		 * 1.7 V floor UFS VCCQ2 is specified for. Nothing votes a
+		 * voltage on this rail (ufshcd only enables it), so the
+		 * constraint has to. The vendor pins it with
+		 * qcom,init-voltage = <1800000>, which mainline has no
+		 * equivalent for.
+		 */
 		pm6125_l11: l11 {
-			regulator-min-microvolt = <1620000>;
-			regulator-max-microvolt = <1980000>;
+			regulator-min-microvolt = <1800000>;
+			regulator-max-microvolt = <1800000>;
 		};
 
 		pm6125_l12: l12 {
